# Interaction System

The Interaction System allows the player to engage with objects, NPCs, and world elements through a consistent and reusable framework.
This system connects player input, UI feedback, and gameplay actions, enabling interaction with core systems such as jobs, Grandpa care, shops, and activities.

---

##  Core Concept

The player interacts with the world through context-sensitive prompts.
When the player looks at or approaches an interactable object or NPC, the system detects it and provides visual feedback.
The player can then trigger an interaction which executes a specific action or opens a contextual menu.

---

##  Interaction Flow

Look at object/NPC → Interaction detected → UI prompt appears → Player inputs interact →  
Action executes OR interaction menu opens

---

##  Detection System

Interaction is based on player targeting:

- Line trace or proximity detection  
- Detects interactable actors in front of the player  
- Only one interactable is active at a time  

### Requirements:
- Actor must be marked as interactable  
- Actor contains interaction logic  

---

##  Interaction UI

### Visual Feedback:
- Hand icon appears when an object can be interacted with  

### Purpose:
- Indicates that an action is available  
- Keeps the UI minimal and non-intrusive  

---

##  Interaction Types

### 1. Instant Interaction

Executes immediately upon input.

#### Examples:
- Picking up items  
- Using medicine  
- Triggering simple actions  

---

### 2. Menu-Based Interaction

Opens a contextual interaction menu with multiple options.

#### Examples:
- Grandpa interaction menu:
  - Talk  
  - Sit  
  - Give Medicine  
  - Feed  
  - Cancel  

- Work terminal:
  - Select shift  

---

##  Interaction Menu System

When interacting with complex objects or NPCs, a menu is displayed.

### Behavior:
- Player movement is disabled  
- Mouse cursor is shown  
- Player selects an option  

### Each option:
- Triggers a specific function  
- May consume time  
- May affect player or world systems  

---

##  System Integration

The Interaction System connects to all major systems:

-  Grandpa System
  - Care actions (medicine, feeding, talking)

-  Job System
  - Work terminal interaction  

-  Shop System
  - Purchasing items  

-  Inventory System
  - Item usage  

-  Player Stats
  - Actions affect stress, morale, stamina  

---

##  Time Integration

Most interactions consume time.

### Examples:
- Sitting with Grandpa → advances time  
- Working → large time increase  
- Activities → moderate time increase  

This reinforces time as a limited resource.

---

##  Feedback System

The system provides immediate feedback for player actions.

### Examples:
- "You do not have any medicine"  
- "Grandpa already took medicine today"  
- Stat changes (e.g. +Health, -Sickness)

### Purpose:
- Inform the player of outcomes  
- Prevent confusion  
- Reinforce system behavior  

---

##  Reusability

The Interaction System is designed to be modular and reusable.

New interactable objects can be added by:

- Marking them as interactable  
- Assigning interaction behavior  
- Connecting them to existing systems  

---

##  Future Expansion (Planned)

### Advanced Menus
- Dialogue systems  
- Branching choices  

### Context Awareness
- Different options based on player state:
  - Inventory  
  - Time of day  
  - Grandpa condition  

---

##  Design Purpose

The Interaction System is designed to:

- Provide a consistent way to interact with the world  
- Connect all gameplay systems together  
- Support both simple and complex actions  
- Maintain immersion while enabling player control  

---

##  Example Scenario

The player approaches Grandpa:

- Hand icon appears  
- Player interacts → menu opens  
- Player selects "Give Medicine"  
- System checks inventory → applies effects → updates UI  

This creates a seamless connection between player input and game systems.
